[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Collision Resolution: Separate Chaining and Open Addressing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In hash tables, a collision occurs when two or more keys hash to the same index. Collision resolution techniques are essential for maintaining the efficiency of hash table operations. Two primary methods for resolving collisions are Separate Chaining and Open Addressing."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Separate Chaining"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Separate chaining uses an array of linked lists (or other dynamic data structures) to handle collisions. Each index in the hash table points to a linked list that stores all the key-value pairs that hash to that index."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When inserting a new key-value pair, hash the key to find the index. Then, add the new pair to the linked list at that index."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When searching for a key, hash the key to find the index. Then, traverse the linked list at that index to find the key."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When deleting a key, hash the key to find the index. Then, traverse the linked list at that index to find and remove the key."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example of Separate Chaining in Java
class HashTable {
    private LinkedList<Entry>[] table;
    private int capacity;

    public HashTable(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        table = new LinkedList[capacity];
        for (int i = 0; i < capacity; i++) {
            table[i] = new LinkedList<>();
        }
    }

    public void put(String key, String value) {
        int index = hash(key);
        table[index].add(new Entry(key, value));
    }

    public String get(String key) {
        int index = hash(key);
        for (Entry entry : table[index]) {
            if (entry.key.equals(key)) {
                return entry.value;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public void remove(String key) {
        int index = hash(key);
        table[index].removeIf(entry -> entry.key.equals(key));
    }

    private int hash(String key) {
        return Math.abs(key.hashCode() % capacity);
    }

    static class Entry {
        String key;
        String value;

        public Entry(String key, String value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Open Addressing"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open addressing resolves collisions by probing for an empty slot within the hash table itself. If a collision occurs, the algorithm probes other locations in the table until an empty slot is found. Common probing techniques include linear probing, quadratic probing, and double hashing."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1 Linear Probing"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linear probing involves checking consecutive slots in the hash table (with wraparound) until an empty slot is found. If a collision occurs at index 'i', the algorithm checks 'i+1', 'i+2', 'i+3', and so on."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insertion: If a collision occurs at index 'i', check 'i+1', 'i+2', etc., until an empty slot is found. Insert the key-value pair at that slot."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Searching: If the key is not found at index 'i', check 'i+1', 'i+2', etc., until the key is found or an empty slot is encountered (indicating the key is not in the table)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deletion: Mark the slot as 'deleted' instead of nulling it out to avoid breaking search sequences. A tombstone approach is often used."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// Example of Linear Probing in Java
class HashTable {
    private String[] keys;
    private String[] values;
    private int capacity;
    private int size;

    public HashTable(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        keys = new String[capacity];
        values = new String[capacity];
        size = 0;
    }

    private int hash(String key) {
        return Math.abs(key.hashCode() % capacity);
    }

    public void put(String key, String value) {
        int index = hash(key);
        while (keys[index] != null) {
            if (keys[index].equals(key)) {
                values[index] = value; // Update existing key
                return;
            }
            index = (index + 1) % capacity; // Linear probing
        }
        keys[index] = key;
        values[index] = value;
        size++;
    }

    public String get(String key) {
        int index = hash(key);
        while (keys[index] != null) {
            if (keys[index].equals(key)) {
                return values[index];
            }
            index = (index + 1) % capacity; // Linear probing
        }
        return null;
    }

    public void remove(String key) {
        int index = hash(key);
        while (keys[index] != null) {
            if (keys[index].equals(key)) {
                keys[index] = "DELETED"; // Use tombstone
                values[index] = null;
                size--;
                return;
            }
            index = (index + 1) % capacity;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"2.2 Quadratic Probing"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Quadratic probing involves checking slots 'i + 1^2', 'i + 2^2', 'i + 3^2', and so on, where 'i' is the initial hash index. This helps to reduce clustering compared to linear probing."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"2.3 Double Hashing"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Double hashing uses a second hash function to determine the step size for probing. If a collision occurs at index 'i', the algorithm checks 'i + hash2(key)', 'i + 2*hash2(key)', 'i + 3*hash2(key)', and so on. This provides better distribution and reduces clustering."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing an appropriate collision resolution technique depends on factors such as the expected load factor of the hash table, the cost of memory allocation, and the desired performance characteristics. Separate chaining is generally simpler to implement, while open addressing can offer better space utilization. However, open addressing can suffer from performance degradation if the table becomes too full."}]
